<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Veldenz</h1><p class="st-meta">1112 CE – 1444 CE (approx.) · capital Veldenz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Moselle and Palatinate county whose heiress carried it to the Wittelsbachs, founding the line of Palatinate-Zweibrücken.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Veldenz appear around 1112 as a branch of the Nahegau counts, taking their name from Veldenz castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernkastel-kues">Bernkastel</a>. Their lands lay scattered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>, along the Moselle and in the western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meisenheim">Meisenheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lauterecken">Lauterecken</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was modest in size, living from vineyards and farming in the hill country, and was held as a fief of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-verdun">Bishopric of Verdun</a>. A second line of counts from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hohengeroldseck">Geroldseck</a> family ruled from 1259. In 1437 the counts also inherited part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Count Frederick III died in 1444 without sons; his daughter Anna had married Stephen, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">King Rupert</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>. Their heirs formed the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-zweibrucken">Palatinate-Zweibrücken</a>, ancestors of the later kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden's</a> Charles X.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-veldenz.jpg" alt="County of Veldenz" width="257" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Heiliges_Römisches_Reich_1400.png: Gustav Droysen derivative work: Beckstet (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheingraf_1400.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1112 CE – 1444 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Veldenz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick III of Veldenz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Moselle Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Veldenz</dd></dl></aside></div>
